heifload's libheif reader callbacks are declared with glib's gint64 where libheif's struct
heif_reader declares int64_t.  The two are normally the same type, but MacPorts' glib2 hard-codes
gint64 as `long` on LP64 (its universal.patch, applied whether or not +universal is set) while
int64_t on Apple is `long long`, and current clang rejects the mismatched function pointers as an
error (-Wincompatible-function-pointer-types).  So libvips fails to build against MacPorts' glib,
which arrives as a dependency of much of MacPorts and is then picked up as the system glib.

Declaring the callbacks with the types libheif's API actually uses is correct whatever glib does,
and changes nothing where gint64 and int64_t already agree.  The bodies pass these values straight
on to vips_source_seek(), a conversion between two signed 64-bit types.

Upstream: https://github.com/libvips/libvips/issues/3901 (the same errors from a misconfigured
Homebrew glib, closed once Homebrew fixed its glib; libvips was not changed).  MacPorts:
https://trac.macports.org/ticket/69549 and https://trac.macports.org/ticket/72748.

